      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Opioids are increasingly prescribed, but there are limited data on opioid receipt by HIV status."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVES",
          "content": "To describe patterns of opioid receipt by HIV status and the relationship between HIV status and receiving any, high-dose, and long-term opioids."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "DESIGN",
          "content": "Cross-sectional analysis of the Veterans Aging Cohort Study."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "PARTICIPANTS",
          "content": "HIV-infected (HIV+) patients receiving Veterans Health Administration care, and uninfected matched controls."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "MAIN MEASURES",
          "content": "Pain-related diagnoses were determined using ICD-9 codes. Any opioid receipt was defined as at least one opioid prescription; high-dose was defined as an average daily dose ≥ 120 mg of morphine equivalents; long-term opioids was defined as ≥ 90 consecutive days, allowing a 30 day refill gap. Multivariable models were used to assess the relationship between HIV infection and the three outcomes."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "KEY RESULTS",
          "content": "Among the HIV+ (n\u2009=\u200923,651) and uninfected (n\u2009=\u200955,097) patients, 31 % of HIV+ and 28 % of uninfected (p\u2009<\u20090.001) received opioids. Among patients receiving opioids, HIV+ patients were more likely to have an acute pain diagnosis (7 % vs. 4 %), but less likely to have a chronic pain diagnosis (53 % vs. 69 %). HIV+ patients received a higher mean daily morphine equivalent dose than uninfected patients (41 mg vs. 37 mg, p\u2009=\u20090.001) and were more likely to receive high-dose opioids (6 % vs. 5 %, p\u2009<\u20090.001). HIV+ patients received fewer days of opioids than uninfected patients (median 44 vs. 60, p\u2009<\u20090.001), and were less likely to receive long-term opioids (31 % vs. 34 %, p\u2009<\u20090.001). In multivariable analysis, HIV+ status was associated with receipt of any opioids (AOR 1.40, 95 % CI 1.35, 1.46) and high-dose opioids (AOR 1.22, 95 % CI 1.07, 1.39), but not long-term opioids (AOR 0.94, 95 % CI 0.88, 1.01)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Patients with HIV infection are more likely to be prescribed opioids than uninfected individuals, and there is a variable association with pain diagnoses. Efforts to standardize approaches to pain management may be warranted in this highly complex and vulnerable patient population."
        }
      ]},
